.\" Man page for scripts(1); the commands, options and exit codes match
.\" `scripts --help` and `scripts describe --format json`, generated from the same tables.
.TH SCRIPTS 1 "2026-11-26" "scripts" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scripts \- statically typed system scripting language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scripts
.B run
.RI [ options ]
.I script
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B scripts
.B check
.RI [ options ]
.I script
.br
.B scripts
.B explain
.RI [ options ]
.I script
.br
.B scripts
.B format
.RI [ options ]
.I script
.br
.B scripts
.B describe
.B \-\-format json
.RI [ \-\-name " NAME" ]
.br
.B scripts
.B \-\-version
.br
.B scripts
.B \-\-help
.br
.B scripts
.B help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scripts
is a statically typed scripting language for Linux system automation. One
native binary (Native AOT, .NET 10) checks, explains and interprets a
.I .scs
file, and needs no .NET runtime at run time. A script is checked before it
runs, data is never re-parsed as code, and a process is launched only through
an explicit request, never through a shell string.
.PP
.BR run ,
.B check
and
.B format --check
accept
.B \-
as the script name to read the script from the standard input; a rewrite
needs a file.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Checks the script, then runs it; nothing runs when a static error exists.
Arguments after the script are exposed to it through
.BR process.arguments .
.TP
.B check
Reports syntax, type, call, import, visibility, mutability, return and
exhaustiveness diagnostics, plus warnings, without running the script.
.TP
.B format
Rewrites the script to the canonical layout (LF, no BOM, final newline, no tab
or trailing space, four spaces per open brace), then checks the result; the
rewrite is idempotent. With
.BR \-\-check ,
reports the violations and rewrites nothing.
.TP
.B explain
Checks the script, then describes it in English, one sentence per statement,
and flags destructive operations.
.TP
.B describe
Writes the complete language description as one JSON document: keywords,
types, functions, groups, error codes, exit codes, limits and grammar.
.TP
.B help
Prints the usage text, like
.BR \-\-help .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-warnings
(
.BR run )
No warning block on standard error; errors still print, and
.B \-\-json\-out
and
.B \-\-errors\-json
keep the warnings. The launcher lints
.B SCRIPTS\-LINT\-0001
and
.B SCRIPTS\-LINT\-0004
are hidden too.
.TP
.B \-\-errors\-json
(
.BR run ,
.BR check )
Diagnostics as JSON on one line: on standard output for
.BR check ,
on standard error for
.BR run .
.TP
.B \-\-json\-out
(
.BR run ,
.BR explain )
No human output; a single JSON document on standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-deny\-warnings
(
.BR check )
Exit 251 as soon as the script has a warning.
.TP
.B \-\-fix
(
.BR check )
Applies the safe fixes carried by the diagnostics to the script; a fix that
cannot be applied safely is not offered.
.TP
.B \-\-module
(
.BR check )
Checks the entry as a module: its
.B public
declarations and imports are allowed. Without the flag, a file that declares
.B public
is refused by
.BR check ,
.B run
and
.BR explain .
.TP
.B \-\-check
(
.B format )
Reports the layout violations and exits 251 without rewriting the script.
Without it
.B format
rewrites the file.
.TP
.B \-\-format json
(
.B describe )
Required: the output is one JSON document.
.TP
.B \-\-name " NAME"
(
.B describe )
Keeps the function entries of a namespace, a module, a receiver type or a
fully qualified name.
.TP
.B \-\-
(
.B run )
Ends the options before the script name, so the name may start with a dash.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Prints the version and exits.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Prints the usage text and exits.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1\-255
The code the script passed to
.BR process.exit_with_code ;
when
.B scripts
itself fails it uses 250\-254.
.TP
.B 250
Command-line usage error.
.TP
.B 251
Static error (syntax, types, modules), or a warning under
.BR \-\-deny\-warnings .
.TP
.B 252
Unreadable or missing script, or a rewrite refused or failed.
.TP
.B 253
Fatal runtime error, or an unhandled error at the top level.
.TP
.B 254
Internal error: a defect in
.BR scripts .
.TP
.B 129, 130, 143
Interrupted by SIGHUP, SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.TP
.B 141
The reader of an output closed the pipe (EPIPE); the exit is silent.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/scripts
The interpreter.
.TP
